 ## Overview
-This article details four specific architectural modifications to the HashNeRF color and density networks, proposed to evaluate the impact of residual connections, convolutional layers, and network depth on reconstruction quality and training time [S1, unofficial]. These variants address the limitations of the standard shallow multi-layer perceptron (MLP) decoder by exploring alternative feature aggregation and information preservation strategies [S1, unofficial]. They are distinct from the base HashNeRF method [8678-hashnerf.md] by altering the internal connectivity and layer types of the feature-decoding stage [S1, unofficial].
+This article details four architectural modifications to the HashNeRF color and density networks, evaluating residual connections, convolutional layers, and network depth on reconstruction quality and training time [S1, unofficial]. These variants modify the standard shallow multi-layer perceptron (MLP) decoder by altering internal connectivity and layer types [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The base HashNeRF method [8678-hashnerf.md] maps spatial coordinates to a high-dimensional feature vector using multi-resolution hash encoding, which is then processed by a shallow MLP. The variants below modify this MLP structure. All variants use the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss over rendered rays [S1, unofficial]. The rendering equation remains:
-$$
-\hat{C}(\mathbf{r}) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} T_i \left( 1 - \exp(-\sigma_i \delta_i) \right) c_i
-$$
-where $T_i = \exp\left(-\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \sigma_j \delta_j\right)$ is the accumulated transmittance, $\sigma_i$ is density, $c_i$ is color, and $\delta_i$ is the segment length [S1, unofficial].
-
-The variants are evaluated under matched iteration budgets (typically 1,000 to 3,000 iterations) on synthetic scenes (e.g., Lego) with reduced view counts (100 training views) [S1, unofficial].
